  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nA weak surface boundary near the KY/TN border will lift north \nacross the southeast half of our region this evening into \ntonight. This occurs as a mid level disturbance across northeast\nAR/southeast MO swings northeastward across our region. A good \namount of clearing has occurred ahead of this wave across KY/TN \nwhich should allow decent instability to develop on the order of\n1500-2000 j/kg MLCAPE by later this afternoon. 0-6 km shear \nisn't as robust as yesterday, but still should peak in the 20-25\nkt range heading into this evening. Thus, the environment may \nbe supportive of a few strong to severe storms. Damaging winds \nwould likely be the main threat, even though DCAPE values likely\nremain below 800 j/kg as low level lapse rates struggle to get \nmuch higher than 7C. Looks like the primary window for severe \nmay be from like 21-03z. \n\nWhile a few lingering showers may occur across our east on Saturday, \nthe main trough axis will be positioned such that the main threat \nshould be primarily east of our cwa. Meanwhile, on Sunday a \ndisturbance passing by to our northwest may allow some weakening \nshowers or storms to sneak into parts of MO or IL either late in the \nday or into the evening/overnight. Still rather low confidence in \nthis though, so PoPs remain silent, but at least a few models are \nshowing this potential.\n\n500mb heights start to rise Sunday as ridging expands northward. The \nridge builds even further to around 597dm by Tuesday into Wednesday. \nThis results in another prolonged period of late season heat for our \nregion, although not quite as extreme as what we have already \nexperienced this month. Pretty good bet that high temperatures reach \n90 degrees for 4 or 5 consecutive days, with Tuesday/Wednesday being \nthe hottest with some areas pushing above 95. Humidity levels remain \nquite high by September standards, with dew points largely in the \nlower 70s. This will result in heat index readings likely exceeding \n100 on Tuesday and Wednesday, and could push near 105 in a few areas.\n\nEnsemble guidance has been in fairly good agreement dropping a cold \nfront through the region later in the week and bringing in a much \ncooler airmass (at or slightly below normal) by next weekend. During \nthe transition a good chance for showers and storms exists Wednesday \nnight into Thursday, possibly lingering into next Friday.",
